.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------- title
\X'ps: exec [/Title (Rate-based ANI and aligned fraction from positional FracMinHash sketches) /Subject (Theory notes for kadence) /Keywords (ANI, aligned fraction, FracMinHash, skani, k-mer) /DOCINFO pdfmark'
.sp 0.2i
.ce 4
.ps 17
.vs 21p
\fBRate-based ANI and aligned fraction\fP
\fBfrom positional FracMinHash sketches\fP
.ps 11
.vs 15p
\fITheory notes for \fBkadence\fI (working name)\fR
16 November 2026
.ps 10.5
.vs 13.5p
.sp 0.6v
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------- body
.H1 "Summary"
The proposed method is promising and has no fatal flaw.
It belongs to the same estimator family as Mash [2] and skani [1]:
ANI $= ( roman "matched" / roman "sketched k-mers inside homologous regions" ) sup {1/k}$.
Only the region finder differs.
skani runs banded two-dimensional chaining, using coordinates on both genomes, inside 20\~kb query chunks;
the proposal runs a one-dimensional run segmentation along one genome's sketch.
Taken literally, the design has four problems, listed below.
Each has a fix that costs one linear scan.
.CAP "Table 1. Issues with the literal design and their fixes" 16
.TS
center tab(;);
cb lb lb lb
c lw(1.95i) lw(1.45i) lw(2.0i).
_
#;Issue;Severity;Fix
_
1;T{
Measuring the match rate per base pair throws away information
T};T{
Moderate: 7\(mu the variance at 99% ANI, 67\(mu at 99.9%
T};T{
Measure the rate per sketched k-mer; the count is known from the stored positions
T}
.sp 0.3v
2;T{
No single fixed patience works from 80% to 99.9% ANI
T};T{
Major, for AF
T};T{
Scale patience to the pair's own match rate (adaptive patience)
T}
.sp 0.3v
3;T{
Regions are defined by the same matches being counted, which biases ANI upward
T};T{
Major below about 90% ANI
T};T{
Truncated-geometric maximum-likelihood estimate of the rate
T}
.sp 0.3v
4;T{
No colinearity check, so spurious and paralogous matches count
T};T{
Major at $k = 15$ below about 95% ANI
T};T{
Use $k >= 17$, or carry B's position and require diagonal consistency
T}
_
.TE
.P
Notation: $a$ is ANI, $k$ the k-mer size, $c$ the FracMinHash scale (one in $c$ k-mers is kept),
$x$ the \fIpatience\fP (consecutive non-matching sketched k-mers before a region closes),
$y$ the \fImin-anchors\fP (matches required to accept a region),
$q = a sup k$, and $s$ the spurious match rate.
Unless stated otherwise, $c = 125$, genomes are 5\~Mbp, and spurious rates assume random sequence.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "0. The method under evaluation"
.LI
\fBSketch.\fP
Each genome is sketched with FracMinHash: a canonical k-mer is kept if and only if its hash is below $2 sup 64 / c$.
The sketch stores the kept k-mers in increasing position order, with contig and strand,
and also a set view of their hashes.
.LI
\fBScan.\fP
To compare genomes A and B, walk A's sketched k-mers in position order and test each for membership in B's sketch.
.LI
\fBRegions.\fP
A match opens a putative homologous region, and further matches extend it.
Once $x$ consecutive sketched k-mers fail to match, the region closes at its last match.
A region is accepted if it contains at least $y$ matches.
(This is how the acceptance rule is interpreted here.)
.LI
\fBANI.\fP
Within accepted regions, ANI is estimated from the rate at which matches occur:
a higher rate means a higher ANI.
.LI
\fBAF.\fP
The fraction of a genome covered by accepted regions is its aligned fraction.
Both A and B need one.
.LE
.P
For comparison, skani keeps matched k-mers with their positions and chains them.
The chaining is a banded dynamic program over coordinates on both genomes,
run in 20\~kb query chunks, and a chain needs at least 3 anchors.
skani then filters to approximately 1-to-1 chains, estimates ANI per chunk as
$( roman "anchors" / roman "seeds" ) sup {1/k}$, and debiases the result with a gradient-boosted regression model.
The proposal replaces chaining with a one-dimensional run rule,
and the per-chunk ratio with a rate estimate.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "1. Model"
FracMinHash keeps a k-mer if and only if $h( roman "kmer" ) < 2 sup 64 / c$, which depends only on the k-mer itself.
So if a sampled k-mer of A is unchanged in B, B sampled it too.
Under independent substitutions:
.LI
Inside a homologous region, each entry of A's position-ordered sketch is a match with probability $q = a sup k$.
The entries are approximately independent, because sampled k-mers rarely overlap when $c \[>>] k$.
.LI
The number of misses $G$ between consecutive matches is geometric,
with $P(G = g) = (1 - q) sup g q$.
.LI
Measured in base pairs, matches form a thinned Poisson process with rate $q / c$.
This is the \(lqPoisson rate\(rq.
.LI
Outside homology, matches occur at a spurious rate $s approx 1 - e sup {-2 |B| / 4 sup k}$ for random sequence.
Real genomes have a higher rate because their k-mer composition is skewed.
.LE
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "2. Use the sketch index as the clock, not base pairs"
The per-base-pair estimator $q hat = m c / L$ has variance $q / n bar$, where $n bar = L / c$.
The count estimator $q hat = m / n$ has variance $q (1 - q) / n$.
Because every sketched position is stored, $n$ is known exactly
(confidence intervals for this count-based FracMinHash setting are derived in [3]).
The variance ratio is $1 / (1 - q)$:
.CAP "Table 2. Variance of the per-bp estimator relative to the count estimator" 9
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb
c c c.
_
ANI;$q$ at $k = 15$;Var(per bp) / Var(count)
_
0.999;0.985;67\(mu
0.99;0.860;7.1\(mu
0.95;0.463;1.9\(mu
0.90;0.206;1.3\(mu
_
.TE
The per-bp rate also assumes uniform sampling density, which repeats, runs of N and
low-complexity sequence violate.
The rate idea still holds, but its clock is the sketch index.
skani also uses counts.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "3. Match density sets the resolution" 16
.CAP "Table 3. Match probability q = a\v'-0.3m'\s-2k\s+2\v'0.3m' and, in brackets, mean distance between matches c/q" 11
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c c.
_
ANI;$k = 15$;$k = 16$;$k = 17$;$k = 19$;$k = 21$
_
0.99;0.860 [0.15 kb];0.852 [0.15 kb];0.843 [0.15 kb];0.826 [0.15 kb];0.810 [0.15 kb]
0.95;0.463 [0.27 kb];0.440 [0.28 kb];0.418 [0.30 kb];0.377 [0.33 kb];0.341 [0.37 kb]
0.90;0.206 [0.61 kb];0.185 [0.67 kb];0.167 [0.75 kb];0.135 [0.93 kb];0.109 [1.14 kb]
0.85;0.087 [1.43 kb];0.074 [1.68 kb];0.063 [1.98 kb];0.046 [2.74 kb];0.033 [3.79 kb]
0.80;0.035 [3.55 kb];0.028 [4.44 kb];0.023 [5.55 kb];0.014 [8.67 kb];0.009 [13.6 kb]
_
.TE
At $c = 125$, region boundaries can be resolved at gene scale (about 1\~kb) only above about 90% ANI.
Every k-mer method at this sampling density has a floor around 80\(en85% ANI;
skani reports reliable ANI at about 82% and above.
Resolution improves linearly as $c$ decreases.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "4. Patience x"
A false break happens when a run of misses inside true homology reaches $x$.
The fraction of a homologous region lost to false breaks is
.EQ
(1 - q) sup x ~ ( q x + 1 - q ) .
.EN
Table\~4 gives the smallest $x$ that keeps this loss at or below 1%.
In base pairs, $x c$ is also the smallest non-homologous island the method can detect.
.CAP "Table 4. Smallest patience x with at most 1% AF loss; in brackets, x\(muc" 11
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c c.
_
ANI;$k = 15$;$k = 16$;$k = 17$;$k = 19$;$k = 21$
_
0.99;3 (0.4 kb);3 (0.4 kb);4 (0.5 kb);4 (0.5 kb);4 (0.5 kb)
0.95;11 (1.4 kb);11 (1.4 kb);12 (1.5 kb);14 (1.8 kb);16 (2.0 kb)
0.90;29 (3.6 kb);32 (4.0 kb);36 (4.5 kb);46 (5.8 kb);57 (7.1 kb)
0.85;72 (9.0 kb);86 (10.8 kb);102 (12.8 kb);142 (17.8 kb);198 (24.8 kb)
0.80;185 (23 kb);232 (29 kb);291 (36 kb);457 (57 kb);716 (90 kb)
_
.TE
\fBThis creates a dilemma.\fP
At $k = 17$, an 85% ANI pair needs $x approx 100$.
Applied to a 99% ANI pair, $x = 100$ bridges every non-homologous island shorter than about 12.8\~kb
and counts it as aligned.
AF is then inflated in strain-level comparisons, where AF matters most.
Going the other way, $x = 4$ is right at 99% ANI but breaks an 85% pair into fragments.
.P
\fBFix: adaptive patience.\fP
Let $alpha$ be the per-gap false-break probability, and set
.EQ
x(q) ~=~ left ceiling { ln alpha } over { ln (1 - q) } right ceiling .
.EN
.CAP "Table 5. Adaptive patience x(q) with \(*a = 0.001" 8
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c c.
_
$k$;ANI 0.99;0.95;0.90;0.85;0.80
_
15;4;12;30;76;193
17;4;13;38;106;304
19;4;15;48;149;476
_
.TE
.ne 7v
Adaptive patience runs in stages:
.NI 1.
Segment with a floor patience $x( q sub roman "floor" )$ taken from \f(CR--min-ani\fP.
.NI 2.
Estimate $q hat$ as in Section\~5.
.NI 3.
Re-segment with $x( q hat )$.
.NI 4.
Repeat steps 2\(en3 until $x$ stops changing, usually within 2\(en3 rounds.
.LE
.P
Each round is one linear scan over a bit vector, so the cost is negligible.
.P
\fBInterpretation.\fP
This rule is exactly the Viterbi decision of a two-state hidden Markov model.
The states are \(lqhomologous\(rq (match probability $q$) and \(lqbackground\(rq ($s approx 0$),
and the state-switch probability is $alpha$.
Leaving the homologous state costs $ln (1 / alpha )$,
and each miss inside it costs $ln ( 1 / (1 - q) )$.
Patience is therefore a principled quantity, not just a knob.
A full HMM is a natural later upgrade:
it would handle local variation in ANI and give posterior region boundaries.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "5. Estimator bias from the segmentation rule"
Each region starts and ends with a match, and by construction every internal gap is shorter than $x$.
So the naive estimate $( m / n ) sup {1/k}$ inside regions is biased upward unless $x \[>>] 1 / q$.
.CAP "Table 6. Naive ANI estimate at k = 17, from theory with gaps pooled across regions" 9
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c.
_
$x$;true 0.95;true 0.90;true 0.85;true 0.80
_
10;0.9511;0.9207;0.9100;0.9064
20;0.9500;0.9050;0.8824;0.8747
40;0.9500;0.9002;0.8613;0.8450
80;0.9500;0.9000;0.8514;0.8203
_
.TE
.CAP "Table 7. Monte Carlo with 400,000 sketched k-mers at k = 17" 9
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb
c c c c.
_
True ANI;$x$;Naive $m/n$;Truncated-geometric MLE
_
0.85;20;0.8969;\fB0.8505\fP
0.85;40;0.8649;\fB0.8499\fP
0.90;15;0.9128;\fB0.8997\fP
0.95;6;0.9571;\fB0.9499\fP
_
.TE
The naive estimate in the Monte Carlo is worse than the pooled-gap theory predicts.
Short regions also contribute a \(lqfree\(rq boundary match each, which the pooled-gap formula ignores.
.P
\fBFix.\fP
Treat the $m - 1$ internal gaps of each accepted region as draws from a geometric distribution
truncated to $G < x$.
The log-likelihood of one gap $g$ is
.EQ
L(q; g) ~=~ g ln (1 - q) ~+~ ln q ~-~ ln ( 1 - (1 - q) sup x ) .
.EN
Setting the score to zero gives $roman E sub q [ G ~ "|" ~ G < x ] = G bar$, the mean observed gap, where
.EQ
roman E sub q [ G ~ "|" ~ G < x ] ~=~ {(1 - q) ( 1 - x (1 - q) sup {x - 1} + (x - 1)(1 - q) sup x )} over {q ( 1 - (1 - q) sup x )} .
.EN
This is monotone in $q$, so bisection solves it.
The Fisher information gives an analytic standard error,
and the delta method turns it into a confidence interval for ANI.
No bootstrap is needed (skani uses one).
This estimator is the cleanest form of the \(lqPoisson rate\(rq idea:
the rate is estimated from the waiting times between matches.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "6. Spurious matches without colinearity"
.CAP "Table 8. Spurious match rate s per sketched k-mer, random 5 Mbp genome, both strands" 9
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c c.
_
$k$;15;16;17;19;21
_
$s$;$9.3 times 10 sup -3$;$2.3 times 10 sup -3$;$5.8 times 10 sup -4$;$3.6 times 10 sup -5$;$2.3 times 10 sup -6$
_
.TE
Spurious-region pressure is $s x$, with $x$ tuned to the ANI floor; it should be well below 0.1.
.CAP "Table 9. Spurious-region pressure s\(mux with x tuned to the ANI floor" 9
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c c.
_
ANI floor;$k = 15$;$k = 16$;$k = 17$;$k = 19$;$k = 21$
_
0.95;0.102;0.026;0.007;0.001;0.000
0.90;0.269;0.074;0.021;0.002;0.000
0.85;\fB0.667\fP;0.200;0.059;0.005;0.000
0.80;\fB1.715\fP;0.540;0.169;0.017;0.002
_
.TE
The expected number of false regions per genome is about $n s ( s x ) sup {y - 1}$, where $n = |A| / c$:
.CAP "Table 10. Expected false regions per 5 Mbp genome, x tuned for 85% ANI" 9
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb cb
c c c c c.
_
$k$ ($x$);$y = 2$;$y = 3$;$y = 4$;$y = 5$
_
15 (72);247;165;110;74
16 (86);18.6;3.7;0.74;0.15
17 (102);1.4;0.08;0.005;$3 times 10 sup -4$
19 (142);0.008;$4 times 10 sup -5$;$2 times 10 sup -7$;$1 times 10 sup -9$
_
.TE
skani's $k = 15$ is safe only because its chaining requires coordinates on both genomes to agree.
Under the pure one-dimensional rule, $k = 15$ works only for ANI of about 95% and above.
There are two options:
.LI
\fB(a) Keep the 1-D rule and raise k to 17\(en19.\fP
skani stores seeds as 32-bit k-mers, which allows $k <= 16$, so seeds would become 64-bit values.
At 90% ANI and above, the variance cost is small, because the standard deviation of the ANI estimate
barely depends on $k$ there.
At 85% ANI, though, the mean gap between matches grows from 1.4\~kb to 2.0\(en2.7\~kb,
so boundaries are resolved less finely.
.LI
\fB(b) Keep k = 15 and carry B's position with each match (\(lqdiagonal mode\(rq).\fP
A match extends the current region only if its diagonal is within a band of the region's running diagonal.
The diagonal is $p sub A - p sub B$, or $p sub A + p sub B$ for reverse-strand matches.
Any other match counts as a miss.
This is still one linear scan, in effect \(lqchaining-lite\(rq.
It removes spurious and paralogous matches, and gives every region an interval on B as well (Section\~8).
.LE
.P
\fBRepeats.\fP
Under the 1-D rule, every copy of a repeat in A matches if any copy exists in B.
This inflates AF for the genome with more repeats;
IS elements and rRNA operons are typically 1\(en3% of a bacterial genome.
Option (b) plus a 1-to-1 overlap filter removes the inflation.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "7. Min-anchors y"
$y$ does two jobs.
First, it rejects spurious regions:
by Table\~10, $y = 3$ gives fewer than 0.1 false regions per genome at $k >= 17$, even with $x approx 100$.
Second, it sets the smallest usable contig, which is about $y c / q$:
.CAP "Table 11. Smallest contig expected to yield y anchors at k = 17" 10
.TS
center tab(;);
cb cb cb cb
c c c c.
_
ANI;$y = 3$;$y = 4$;$y = 5$
_
0.99;0.4 kb;0.6 kb;0.7 kb
0.95;0.9 kb;1.2 kb;1.5 kb
0.90;2.2 kb;3.0 kb;3.7 kb
0.85;5.9 kb;7.9 kb;9.9 kb
0.80;16.7 kb;22.2 kb;27.8 kb
_
.TE
$y = 3$ matches skani's minimum chain size.
At 85% ANI, contigs shorter than about 6\~kb add nothing to AF whatever $y$ is.
This is inherent to $c = 125$; fragmented MAGs need a lower $c$.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "8. The two-pass AF problem"
\fBWhat skani does.\fP
Its chains are approximately 1-to-1.
It counts aligned bases on the query once and divides that count by both genome lengths,
so AF for the reference is the aligned query bases divided by the reference length.
This is cheap, but correct only to the extent the chains really are 1-to-1.
.P
\fBWhat this method does.\fP
The expensive step, finding shared k-mers, is symmetric, so the two sketches are intersected once:
.NI 1.
Merge-join the two hash-sorted arrays.
This is cache-friendly; skani's \f(CR\%get_anchors\fP also uses a galloping merge-join rather than hash lookups.
.NI 2.
For each shared hash, set a bit for each of its entries in both genomes' position-ordered arrays,
using the stored ranks.
.NI 3.
Segment each genome with one linear scan over its bit vector.
.LE
.P
The total cost is $O( n sub A + n sub B )$.
No 1-to-1 assumption is needed, and the AF of A and of B are each exact under the method's own definition.
In diagonal mode, each region already carries an interval on both genomes.
A single scan then gives both AFs, and overlapping projections onto B provide a 1-to-1 filter.
.P
\fBBonus: symmetric ANI.\fP
Pooling gaps from both sides makes ANI(A, B) = ANI(B, A) exactly.
skani is asymmetric: it chooses which genome is the query
using a score of genome length times mean contig length.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "9. Caveats shared with every k-mer ANI method"
.LI
\fBHeterogeneous divergence.\fP
By Jensen's inequality, $roman E [ a sup k ] > ( roman E [a] ) sup k$.
Pooled k-mer ANI therefore overestimates alignment ANI when divergence varies along the genome.
Per-region estimates help.
skani corrects this with a gradient-boosted model trained against ANIm.
.LI
\fBIndels\fP destroy k-mers, but alignment ANI ignores them, so k-mer ANI runs slightly low.
.LI
\fBFragmented MAGs.\fP
Regions cannot cross contig ends (Section\~7).
.LI
\fBANI floor\fP of about 80\(en85% at $c = 125$ (Section\~3).
.LE
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "10. Proposed defaults" 17
These defaults are pending the open decisions listed in ROADMAP.md.
.CAP "Table 12. Proposed defaults" 12
.TS
center tab(;);
lb lb lb lb
l l lw(1.9i) l.
_
Parameter;Flag;Default;Basis
_
$k$;\f(CR-k\fP;T{
17 in 1-D mode, 15 in diagonal mode
T};Section 6
$c$;\f(CR-c\fP;125;skani default, Section 3
patience $x$;\f(CR--patience\fP;T{
adaptive, with an integer override
T};Section 4
false-break probability $alpha$;\f(CR--break-alpha\fP;$10 sup -3$;Section 4
ANI floor for stage 1;\f(CR--min-ani\fP;T{
0.85 ($x approx 102$ at $k = 17$, 72 at $k = 15$)
T};Sections 3, 4
min-anchors $y$;\f(CR--min-anchors\fP;3;Section 7
_
.TE
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.H1 "Reproducibility"
.ad l
Every number in these notes is produced by \f(CR\%analysis/theory.py\fP,
which uses only the Python standard library.
Run it with \f(CR\%python3\ \%analysis/theory.py\fP.
The Monte Carlo in Table\~7 uses 400,000 simulated sketched k-mers with a fixed random seed.
.ad b
.H1 "References"
.de RF
.sp 0.35v
.in 0.3i
.ti -0.3i
\\$1\h'0.3i-\w'\\$1'u'\c
..
.RF [1]
J. Shaw and Y. W. Yu.
Fast and robust metagenomic sequence comparison through sparse chaining with skani.
\fINature Methods\fP 20, 1661\(en1665 (2023).
.RF [2]
B. D. Ondov et al.
Mash: fast genome and metagenome distance estimation using MinHash.
\fIGenome Biology\fP 17, 132 (2016).
.RF [3]
M. R. Hera, N. T. Pierce-Ward and D. Koslicki.
Deriving confidence intervals for mutation rates across a wide range of evolutionary distances using FracMinHash.
\fIGenome Research\fP 33, 1061\(en1068 (2023).
.LE
